  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The ice at Dawn Naong made a sound when you cracked its memory-layer. Not the clean fracture-tone the manuals described—a bright harmonic, a struck bell thinning into silence. This was wet. A slow exhale, like something sealed for too long finally letting go. The first time Fimalin heard it, she pulled her flipper back and held it there, suspended, the crystal stylus still humming against her wrist.
    
    Frost-ward Silvmococriaa~ Tier, standing three body-lengths away in the dimness between vault-spires, flinched at the same sound. She covered it by adjusting the fire-core pouch at her throat. But Fimalin saw. The Nightshelf woman's whole posture changed—shoulders drawn in, weight shifted back—and then she was still again, watching Fimalin with an expression that offered nothing.
    
    Fimalin did not ask. Some sounds carried history she hadn't earned.
    
    They had been in the vault for six hours. The aurora-light came through the crystal galleries at wrong angles now—the spires had shifted over decades of thermal instability, and half the refraction channels were occluded with a yellowish film that smelled faintly of sulfur. Fimalin had spent the first two hours mapping the spectral drift, chalking positions on the ice floor with a shorthand ☽'mortoth couldn't read and Silvmococriaa~ wouldn't look at.
    
    "Third-position refraction is eighteen degrees west of standard," Fimalin said. "The bloom-trace should register at the intersection of the second and fifth harmonics, but the corruption is producing a false resonance at—"
    
    "The vein coughs," Silvmococriaa~ said.
    
    Fimalin stopped. "I don't know what that means."
    
    Silvmococriaa~ looked at ☽'mortoth, who was crouched near the sealed lower-vault door, running a bare flipper along the seam where corruption-discoloration met clean ice. He didn't look up.
    
    "The fire beneath this place," Silvmococriaa~ said. She paused. Tried again. "There is a reaction. Below. The taint is not static. It responds to—" She stopped. Touched the fire-core amulet at her throat—a gesture Fimalin was learning to recognize: consultation with doctrine, checking what she was permitted to say against what needed saying.
    
    "It responds to the ice being opened," ☽'mortoth finished. He stood, brushing frost from his flippers. "When you read the memory-layer, the corruption reads you back."
    
    This was the problem. The Shell Hatch required pristine ice-memory: the electromagnetic ghost of a krill bloom's passage, frozen into the substrate, legible through aurora-charged crystal arrays. At Dawn Naong, three seasons after the Tide End Incident had bled corruption outward from the Glow-Fissure, the memory-layers were fouled. Fimalin's crystals picked up the bloom-traces—the faint harmonic in her chest, the brine-taste at the back of her tongue—but the signal was wrapped in something else. A warmth where there should be none. A resonance that didn't correspond to any krill pattern in the archive.
    
    She couldn't strip the corruption from the reading. She didn't know its shape.
    
    The Nightshelf knew its shape. They'd lived in it.
    
    ---
    
    ☽'mortoth held a fire-core against the corrupted ice and Fimalin watched what happened to the light.
    
    The core was uncut—rough, the size of a penguin's closed fist, radiating a steady heat she could feel from where she stood. When he pressed it to the ice, the yellow film in the nearest crystal spire shifted. Not much. A change in opacity, a slight deepening of color, as though the corruption were responding to the fire-core's proximity by becoming more itself.
    
    "There," Fimalin said. "The spectral position just moved. That reads as a second harmonic, but it shouldn't be."
    
    "What does it look like?"
    
    "Amber. Concentrated at the base of the spire, flowing upward. If I mapped it, it would register as a bloom-trace, but the pattern doesn't match any organism."
    
    "That is the flaw," Silvmococriaa~ said. She had come closer—still not close enough to touch, maintaining the precise distance that meant *I am here under protest*. "What you see in the light is what we feel in the heat. Your instrument shows it. Our instrument shows it. They are—" She searched for the word.
    
    "Two names for the same flaw in the ice," ☽'mortoth said.
    
    Fimalin knelt and recalibrated the nearest crystal array, her flipper-tips finding the tuning-grooves by touch. The crystal hummed against her—a frequency too low to hear, felt through bone. She adjusted the resonance downward, toward the register where the false harmonic lived, and the aurora-light in the spire dimmed as the crystal pulled energy to match.
    
    "I can tune to the corruption," she said. "I can isolate its spectral position. But I can't read it. I can see it. I can't interpret what it does."
    
    "We can read it," Silvmococriaa~ said. "We cannot see it."
    
    The silence that followed was the entire problem.
    
    "Then show me the thermal map," Fimalin said. "If I can overlay the gradient shift onto the spectral—"
    
    "No."
    
    The word came from ☽'mortoth. He stepped forward—not into the light, but to its edge.
    
    "She speaks of the vein's behavior. I will not have our gradient data inscribed on an Aurora slate and carried to the surface for open discussion. That is not how this works."
    
    "Then how does this work? I need to understand the distortion to tune around it."
    
    "You need to *tune around it*," ☽'mortoth said. "You do not need to understand it."
    
    "Then we're finished," Fimalin said, "and the shallows stay dead."
    
    The corrupted crystal hummed its wrong note into the silence. Somewhere deep in the ice, the Glow-Fissure pulsed—low, rhythmic, like breathing. Silvmococriaa~ flinched. The motion was small, controlled, and she did not explain it.
    
    Fimalin gathered her crystal stylus and her chalked notations and climbed the stairwell to the upper gallery. She sat among the refraction channels and retuned crystals that didn't need retuning and tasted brine at the back of her tongue from the reading she'd already spent herself on, and she waited for the sound of separate tunnels carrying separate footsteps away.
    
    ---
    
    It was Silvmococriaa~ who came back.
    
    An hour later. Alone. She appeared at the top of the stairwell with a fire-core in her flipper—a small one, dull red, still warm enough to make the air shimmer. The smell reached Fimalin first: iron and sulfur, volcanism compressed into a stone.
    
    "Hold this against the ice," Silvmococriaa~ said. "Here." She indicated a point on the gallery floor. "Then run your reading."
    
    "What will I see?"
    
    "What the heat finds." A pause. The fire-core amulet at Silvmococriaa~'s throat shifted as she breathed. "The corruption maps onto thermal boundaries. This is—we know this. We have always known this. Where the heat changes, the taint behaves differently. Your crystal reads the memory-layer through light. The light hits the taint and scatters. But if the heat defines the boundary first—"
    
    "The light would follow the thermal contour. Read the memory beneath the corruption instead of through it." Fimalin stared at the fire-core. "You can map the corruption's shape by temperature alone?"
    
    "We live in it," Silvmococriaa~ said. No pride. No bitterness. A statement of material fact.
    
    Whatever had been decided between Silvmococriaa~ and ☽'mortoth in the hour Fimalin had spent pretending to work—whatever it had cost the Weal' Clique~ woman to come back on her own terms, carrying a piece of the Nightshelf's heat in her bare flipper—Fimalin would not learn. The negotiation had been a Nightshelf decision with a Nightshelf cost.
    
    She placed the fire-core against the ice. The heat bled into it—not uniformly, but in patterns, fingers of warmth following existing fractures, and the corruption shrank from each one. Not far. Not permanently. But measurably.
    
    She charged the crystal. The aurora-pulse entered the ice, hit the thermal boundary Silvmococriaa~'s fire-core had carved, and *followed it*—bending with the heat-contour, sliding beneath the corrupted layer into cleaner memory beneath.
    
    What came back was faint, fragmentary, and unmistakably a krill-bloom trace.
    
    "There," Fimalin whispered. The somatic reading hit her chest—faint pressure, the ghost-taste of brine sharpening until it was almost flavor. "Three points off the southern feeding ground, maybe—the trace is partial, but it's real."
    
    Silvmococriaa~ crouched beside her and pressed her own flipper to the ice. "The heat finds the edge. Your light reads what's inside it."
    
    ---
    
    The work took nine hours over two days. They slept in shifts in the back chamber—Fimalin never at the same time as both Nightshelf operatives, a precaution none of them acknowledged.
    
    They fought over sequencing. Fimalin wanted to pulse the array simultaneously—standard Shell Hatch protocol, every crystal firing in harmonic. Silvmococriaa~ insisted on cascading the fire-cores first, letting the thermal map establish before the light entered. The compromise was ugly: a staggered pulse, fire then light, with a half-count gap that felt wrong to both of them.
    
    "Smaller increments," Silvmococriaa~ said, adjusting a fire-core by feel where Fimalin couldn't detect a difference. "The boundary has to breathe. You're tuning it rigid."
    
    "Rigid tuning produces repeatable readings."
    
    "Rigid tuning in clean ice. This ice is sick. It moves."
    
    On the second day they read seven memory-layers. Each time Silvmococriaa~ held a fire-core to corrupted ice, Fimalin saw the cost in the Nightshelf woman's face—not physical pain but something more precise, a tightening around the eyes that matched the moment the core's reserves dimmed. Each burn spent fire that could have heated a family's chamber for a week.
    
    And the Shell Hatch took what it always took from the reader. As the krill-bloom traces sharpened in the ice below, Fimalin's own recent memories softened. The brine-taste grew stronger, crowding out the rest. She forgot what she'd eaten that morning. By afternoon she lost a word—her own name, briefly—and found it again only after pressing her flippers flat against cold ice and breathing. By evening she couldn't remember arriving at Dawn Naong, only being here, flippers on the crystal, the ice whispering its recovered secrets.
    
    She wrote the readings down before she lost them.
    
    Silvmococriaa~ watched her struggle to recall the designation for the second crystal array. "That is the cost," she said.
    
    "I know what the cost is."
    
    "You know what the manuals say the cost is. You do not know what it becomes."
    
    Fimalin didn't argue. She tasted brine and the ghost of a krill swarm that had passed this place before she was born, and she kept working.
    
    ☽'mortoth contributed fire-core alignment data in oblique fragments—never a complete picture, only enough to adjust the current placement. He made marks on the wall in a notation that was not letters, strokes grouped in threes. Once, Fimalin saw him consult with Silvmococriaa~ in coded Nightshelf cant, rapid and low, and Silvmococriaa~'s response was a single syllable that might have been permission or might have been warning. The information that followed—a precise thermal reading of the sub-floor gradient—was delivered as though it had always been common knowledge.
    
    ---
    
    The final array was a thing neither tradition would have built. Aurora crystals spaced at intervals determined by Nightshelf heat-mapping. Fire-core embers seated in crystal housings that Fimalin carved and Silvmococriaa~ positioned by feel. The light came from above, the heat from below, and where they intersected the corrupted ice became briefly transparent—not clean, not healed, but readable. A window burned-and-lit through the taint.
    
    It produced a krill-bloom prediction for the eastern shallows. Feeding grounds that hadn't appeared on any aurora-only survey, bloom-timing patterns that corresponded not to aurora-cycles but to volcanic pulse. The hybrid technique found what neither tradition had known to look for—a layer of the ice that aurora-crystals alone would overshoot and fire-core readings alone would misinterpret.
    
    The confidence was low by Aurora standards. ☽'mortoth asked what it would take to raise it and Fimalin said more memory-layers and more fire-cores and more sessions and more of her own recall fed into the reading, and ☽'mortoth said, "That is not a price we set today."
    
    They dismantled the array. Silvmococriaa~ kept the fire-core embers. Fimalin kept the tuning-maps. ☽'mortoth kept his tally on the wall, which neither woman was permitted to photograph or transcribe.
    
    The prediction held. The bloom came, thin but present, eleven days after the reading placed it—east of Dawn Naong, shallow current, the first krill in those waters in three seasons. Not abundance. Sufficiency.
    
    The array's design existed in no single document. Half lived in Fimalin's spectral charts. Half lived in Silvmococriaa~'s memory and ☽'mortoth's wall-marks and the institutional silence of Mandatum∴absconditu. To rebuild it, you would need both. To improve it, you would need both in the same room again, and nothing guaranteed that would happen.
    
    It worked. It wasn't theirs. It was the thing between them—specific, provisional, composite—and it sat in the ice at Dawn Naong like a scar that could see.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771734677726_0", anchor: "This was wet. A slow exhale, like something sealed for too long finally letting go.", text: "I have heard this sound. Stratum six, Foundation Depths, where the ice transitions from blue to something darker. The manuals do not describe it because the manuals were written by penguins who worked in clean ice. In corrupted layers, the memory-substrate has absorbed moisture from sources that are not glacial. When you crack it, what escapes is not air. I never identified what it was. I did not try twice.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734677726_1", anchor: "the corruption reads you back", text: "☽'mortoth states this as operational fact — a hazard to work around. I want the reader to sit with it longer than the chronicler does. The deep Foundation ice contains impressions from presences that predate penguins. Those impressions are structured. They are attentive. What ☽'mortoth describes — a reciprocal awareness in corrupted ice responding to being read — is not a metaphor for danger. It is a description of something looking back. I noted in my Book of Silence annotation that what lives in the deep does not instruct. It watches. Here it is, watching through the taint at Dawn Naong, and three penguins are too busy arguing about spectral harmonics to be properly afraid.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734677726_2", anchor: "strokes grouped in threes", text: "Mandatum∴absconditu base-three encoding. I have seen fragments of this system in administrative ice — always partially effaced, always incomplete. The grouping is deliberate: no single stroke-set contains enough information to reconstruct the whole without the adjacent sets. The notation is designed to be unintelligible in isolation. ☽'mortoth is not taking notes. He is distributing a secret across a surface he controls.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734677726_3", anchor: "Whatever had been decided between Silvmococriaa~ and ☽'mortoth in the hour Fimalin had spent pretending to work", text: "I will tell you what it cost, because the chronicler is too polite and Fimalin was not permitted to know. Weal' Clique~ lost ☽'slew Nisaemesius~ during the Clever Ice Age — the wound that defines them. Their stewardship of the Glow-Fissure is not a position of strength; it is a consolation they were handed because no one else wanted to tend a bleeding corruption source. Silvmococriaa~ came back alone carrying a fire-core and thermal knowledge that Mandatum∴absconditu classifies as existential intelligence. She bought that right with something. Given what the Clique~ has left to spend, I suspect she traded future Fissure access — the one thing they hold that anyone wants. She came back lighter than she left, and not because of the fire-core.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734677726_4", anchor: "Each burn spent fire that could have heated a family's chamber for a week", text: "This figure recurs in Clever Ice Age accounts as a standard unit of fire-core cost. It is roughly accurate for an uncut core of the size described — the Rofundis∴rupt hadn't collapsed yet, and core caloric density was higher in this period than post-Incursion. But notice what the chronicler is doing: translating Nightshelf strategic resources into Aurora domestic units. The Nightshelf do not measure fire-core expenditure in family-chambers-per-week. They measure it in tunnel-hours and forge-capacity. The conversion is for us.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771734677726_5", anchor: "bloom-timing patterns that corresponded not to aurora-cycles but to volcanic pulse", text: "This is the discovery that should have rewritten both traditions' understanding of krill migration, and as far as I can determine from the archive, it did not. The finding implies that the deep volcanic systems beneath the Berg drive biological cycles that surface aurora-observation entirely misses — that the Nightshelf's fire and the Aurora's light are not merely complementary trade goods but complementary epistemologies, each blind to half the system. The implications for the Flipper Accord alone are staggering. That this insight was produced by two diminished factions with no institutional backing, preserved in no unified document, and apparently never replicated, is — I am trying to find the scholarly term. The scholarly term is 'heartbreaking.'", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734677726_6", anchor: "The array's design existed in no single document", text: "And so the most important technical innovation of the mid-Clever Ice Age survives as a krill-bloom prediction that came true once, a spectral chart in an Aurora archive no one indexes, wall-marks in a vault at Dawn Naong that corruption has probably consumed by now, and whatever Silvmococriaa~ carried in her memory until she stopped carrying anything. I catalogue records for a living. I am, it appears, cataloguing the absence of one. If my annotated edition is ever printed — which, given 'the transition,' seems optimistic — let this note serve as the record that the record does not exist.", type: :tangent, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771734677726_7", anchor: "thin but present, eleven days after the reading placed it", text: "Eleven days. Not ten, not fourteen. I want to trust this because the specificity earns it, but a single successful prediction from a novel technique is not validation — it is an anecdote. The bloom could have been residual, pushed east by current-shift rather than predicted by ice-memory. I note this not because I doubt the technique worked, but because no one was ever given the chance to test whether it worked *again*.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
